from ipatch import apply
apply([
("On its own that pays only when his next spell or an ally's attack deals that type. Once Aspera also carries a rune of the same type, such as Flaming, every Strike in that window sets off the weakness.", "By itself that helps only when his next spell or an ally's attack deals that type. Once Aspera also carries a rune of the same type, such as Flaming, every Strike made while the weakness lasts benefits from it."),
("Fearsome does more for the whole party from the same trigger, so this is a later rune, at its best as the greater version beside an energy rune of the same type.", "Fearsome does more for all his allies on the same critical hit, so Energy Vulnerability is a rune for later levels, and it is at its best as the greater version beside an energy rune of the same type."),
("Against undead it out-damages the other runes of its level, since persistent damage keeps working between his turns. Against everything else it does nothing, so it holds a slot on Aspera only while undead are the main enemy, and waits on a runestone when they are not.", "Against undead it deals more damage than the other runes of its level, because persistent damage continues between his turns. Against everything else it does nothing, so it belongs on Aspera only while undead are the main enemy and is kept on a runestone when they are not."),
("Against unholy enemies it is the strongest rune at its level; against others its 1d4 is less than Frost or Flaming add.", "Against unholy enemies it is the strongest rune at its level, while against others its 1d4 is less than Frost or Flaming adds."),
("In a large party that is worth more damage than any die a rune adds to his own Strike,", "With several allies attacking, that is worth more damage than any die a rune adds to his own Strike,"),
("The rune limits the bonus to allies who share an alignment component with him, so which allies it reaches is for the GM to settle. Greater Fearsome is stronger against a single enemy that can be frightened; Hopeful is the pick when enemies are many or fearless.", "The rune limits the bonus to allies who share an alignment component with him, so it may not reach every ally. Greater Fearsome is stronger against a single enemy that can be frightened, and Hopeful is the better rune when enemies are numerous or immune to fear."),
("Against anything else Vitality Lash has no target, so neither benefit can be switched on, and Clay Sphere is the better attachment.", "Against anything else Vitality Lash has no target, so neither benefit can be obtained, and Clay Sphere is the better attachment."),
("Clay Sphere still offers more: all three physical types and a daily Enlarge. The barbell is the alternative when a ranged cantrip matters more than those.", "Clay Sphere still offers more, namely all three physical damage types and a daily Enlarge, so the barbell is the alternative when a ranged cantrip matters more than those."),
("On Aspera, Lightweave Scarf turns its daily Hypnotize into a setup: after that cast, his next Strike that hits leaves the target confused for a round, with no saving throw.", "On Aspera, Lightweave Scarf adds an effect to its daily Hypnotize: after that casting, his next Strike that hits leaves the target confused for a round, with no saving throw."),
("Whether the Strike inside a Spellstrike of Hypnotize counts as that next Strike is for the GM to decide; otherwise it costs a cast on one turn and a Strike by the end of the next.", "If the Strike inside a Spellstrike of Hypnotize does not count as that next Strike, the effect takes a casting on one turn and a Strike by the end of the next."),
("so the greater Phantasmal Doorknob gives more over a day and the scarf gives more in the one fight that matters.", "so the greater Phantasmal Doorknob gives more over a whole day and the scarf gives more in the day's most important fight."),
("so it is a sound spell for a Spellstrike on a turn without a slot. On the armour, that cast lets him Step as a free action once a round until the end of his next turn: a reach fighter can strike and step back out of an enemy's reach, or shift to a flank, without spending an action.", "so it is a sound spell for a Spellstrike on a turn when he is not spending a slot. On the armour, casting it lets him Step as a free action once a round until the end of his next turn, so he can strike and step back out of an enemy's reach, or move to a flank, without spending an action."),
("The armour version is the better use, and the strongest candidate for the armour's attachment at the levels where cantrip turns are common.", "The armour version is the better use, and it is the strongest candidate for the armour's attachment at the levels where he often casts cantrips."),
("Because it lasts through his next turn, one cantrip turn in two keeps it up for a whole fight, and resistance to every physical hit is the defence a front-liner uses most. The cost is the spell itself: Scatter Scree is a small Reflex cantrip, and through Spellstrike it affects only the target, so those turns give up a slot spell. It suits the levels where cantrip turns are common and fades once he has a slot for every round.", "Because it lasts through his next turn, casting the cantrip on one turn in two maintains it for a whole fight, and resistance to every physical hit is the protection someone fighting at the front uses most. The cost lies in the spell itself, because Scatter Scree is a weak Reflex cantrip that affects only the target in a Spellstrike, and each turn spent on it is a turn without a stronger spell. It suits the levels at which he often casts cantrips and loses its value once he has a slot to spend in every round."),
("That is a round of real protection, but it needs the cantrip every turn, and Void Warp targets only living creatures, so against anything else there is no cast to set it off.", "That is substantial protection for a round, but it needs the cantrip every turn, and Void Warp targets only living creatures, so against anything else the spell cannot be cast to obtain it."),
("which a reach fighter's enemies rarely do. It is a passive armour attachment that asks nothing of his turns, and the modest size of the bonus is its limit.", "which his enemies rarely do when he fights at reach. As an armour attachment it works without any action from him, and its limit is the modest size of the bonus."),
("a store of healing set up before a fight that he and his allies draw on by touch. Against undead the weapon version is an extra rune for nothing; against other enemies it gives up the Phantasmal Doorknob's blindness for no gain, so it goes on Aspera only when undead are expected.", "which places a store of healing before a fight that he and his allies can draw on by touch. Against undead the weapon version amounts to an extra rune at no cost in slots. Against other enemies it gives up the Phantasmal Doorknob's blindness and gains nothing, so it goes on Aspera only when undead are expected."),
("It is a quiet armour attachment that adds one good spell a day.", "As an armour attachment it asks nothing of his turns and adds one good spell a day."),
("That makes it a natural armour attachment once the greater Phantasmal Doorknob takes Aspera's slot at 10: he keeps Gouging Claw without a cantrip slot and gains a standing answer to invisibility. Clay Sphere on the armour offers a daily Enlarge instead. The weapon effect and the daily Dinosaur Form are for a battle-form user and do nothing for him.", "That makes it a natural armour attachment once the greater Phantasmal Doorknob takes Aspera's slot at 10, since he keeps Gouging Claw without a cantrip slot and gains a permanent means of locating invisible creatures. Clay Sphere on the armour offers a daily Enlarge instead. The weapon effect and the daily Dinosaur Form are meant for a character who fights in a battle form and do nothing for him."),
("It is the cheap first banner.", "It is the inexpensive banner to start with."),
("Most of a party's critical hits are against off-guard targets, so it triggers nearly as often as Blazing Banner, but one extra die is less than persistent fire that keeps burning.", "Most critical hits are scored against off-guard targets, so it applies nearly as often as Blazing Banner, but one extra die is worth less than persistent fire that keeps burning."),
("In a large party one action buys more Hit Points than any heal of its level, provided an area attack or several enemy turns follow in that round. The action is the cost, in a class with none to spare. The activation asks for the banner to be held; whether wielding the weapon it is affixed to counts is for the GM.", "With many allies in range, one action provides more Hit Points than any healing of its level, provided an area attack or several enemy turns follow in that round. The cost is the action, which a magus can rarely spare. The activation also requires the banner to be held, and it is not certain that wielding the weapon it is affixed to satisfies that."),
("The action is the one he would spend recharging Spellstrike, so it fits the turns when the party can pile on. Like Stalwart's Banner, its activation asks for the banner to be held.", "The action is the one he would spend recharging Spellstrike, so it suits the turns when several allies can attack the same target. Like Stalwart's Banner, its activation requires the banner to be held."),
("It is passive, so on Aspera it works from the first round without an action, and it covers the whole party against the area spells that hurt a group most.", "It needs no activation, so on Aspera it works from the first round without an action, and it protects all his allies against the area spells that hurt a group most."),
("For a party without that spell it is 5 feet for everyone at no action; for Cyrus with a wand of Tailwind it is spent on the allies alone.", "Allies without that spell gain 5 feet of Speed at no cost in actions, while Cyrus, if he uses a wand of Tailwind, gains nothing himself."),
("That frees the party to fight inside its own terrain spells and removes one of the commonest ways an enemy slows a melee line. Its worth depends on how often difficult terrain appears, including the terrain his own area spells leave behind; where it does, nothing else gives the whole party the same freedom.", "That lets his side fight inside the terrain its own spells create and removes one of the commonest ways in which an enemy slows those fighting in melee. Its worth depends on how often difficult terrain appears, including the terrain his own area spells leave behind, and where it does appear nothing else gives all his allies the same freedom."),
("That is a second Stride for nothing in most fights, the same gain as the Tactician's Helm's best option without needing a charge, and it is not invested. The item is a pair of prosthetic legs, so whether Cyrus can use one is for the GM to decide. If he can, it is among the best purchases at its level for a class that is always an action short.", "In most fights that gives him a second Stride at no cost, which is the same gain as the best option of the Tactician's Helm without needing a charge, and it is not invested. The item is made as a pair of prosthetic legs, so for Cyrus it would have to take another form. In such a form it is among the best purchases at its level for a magus, who is always an action short."),
("Small persistent effects, the 1d4 and 1d6 that runes, poisons and bleeds add, mostly end on their first tick; larger ones are only reduced. It is invested, and Kindler Cameo already ends persistent damage once a day, so it earns a slot only while investment is to spare.", "Small persistent effects, such as the 1d4 and 1d6 that runes, poisons and bleeding add, mostly end the first time they would deal damage, while larger ones are only reduced. It is invested, and Kindler Cameo already ends persistent damage once a day, so it is worth wearing only while he has investment to spare."),
("A free Translocate saves a rank-4 slot, and arriving concealed makes the escape or the approach safer. It also gives +2 to Deception. It takes the cloak slot at the level where Cloak of Illusions stops being useful.", "A Translocate that costs no slot saves one of rank 4, and arriving concealed makes an escape or an approach safer. It also gives +2 to Deception. It is worn as a cloak, and it becomes available at the level where the Cloak of Illusions stops being useful."),
("It matters to a character who jumps and teleports around high ground, and stops mattering once Fly is routine.", "It is useful to someone who jumps and teleports around high ground, and loses its value once he can cast Fly regularly."),
("Thrasher Tail is a reaction when Cyrus is grabbed: 8d6 slashing damage to the grabber against a fixed basic Reflex DC of 25, and he is no longer grabbed whatever the result.", "Thrasher Tail gives a reaction when Cyrus is grabbed, which deals 8d6 slashing damage to the creature holding him against a fixed basic Reflex DC of 25 and frees him whatever the result."),
("so the tail is for fights where he has no Focus Point or cannot see a space to teleport to. It is a prosthetic tail, so its use is for the GM to decide.", "so the tail is for fights in which he has no Focus Point or cannot see a space to teleport to. It is made as a prosthetic tail, so for Cyrus it would have to take another form."),
("That is flight in most fights without a slot or two actions of casting: enough to reach a flying enemy or cross a gap and land.", "That gives him flight in most fights without a slot or two actions of casting, which is enough to reach a flying enemy or to cross a gap and land."),
("so in practice the resistance is always up. Against enemies who cast divine spells it is the largest standing defence he can buy at 12; against everyone else it does nothing. It is invested, so its place depends on how often divine casters are the enemy.", "so in practice the resistance is always in effect. Against enemies who cast divine spells it is the largest constant protection he can buy at 12, and against everyone else it does nothing. It is invested, so whether it is worth wearing depends on how often he faces divine spellcasters."),
("Silent Heart is a reaction, once an hour, for a +2 status bonus to a save against an emotion effect or one that would leave him controlled, used before the roll. Those are the saves a front-liner can least afford to fail, and once an hour means once in most fights.", "Once an hour, Silent Heart lets Cyrus use a reaction before rolling to gain a +2 status bonus to a save against an emotion effect or one that would leave him controlled. Those are the saves that someone fighting at the front can least afford to fail, and once an hour means once in most fights."),
("The reaction is the one Reactive Strike uses, and the bonus does not add to another status bonus to saves. It is invested and cheap.", "It uses the reaction he also wants for Reactive Strike, and the bonus does not add to another status bonus to saves. It is invested and inexpensive."),
("For a front-liner it is insurance against the one bad round, and it asks for no action. It is invested, which is its real cost.", "For someone who fights at the front it guards against a single disastrous round, and it needs no action. Its real cost is the investment it takes up."),
("for two actions; the greater version at 14 works once an hour.", "for two actions, and the greater version at 14 works once an hour."),
("while the light lasts; those spells also reach them at twice their range. That turns one cast into a setup for his Will spells against a group. The costs are a hand for the wand, two actions for the cast and a turn before the spell it sets up, so it suits a planned fight opened from range and not a turn in melee.", "while the light lasts, and those spells also reach them at twice their range. One casting therefore makes his Will spells more likely to succeed against a whole group. It needs a hand for the wand, two actions for the casting and a turn before the spell that benefits, so it suits a planned fight begun from range and not a turn in melee."),
("Spellstriker Staff is a staff made for the magus: when a Spellstrike with a slot spell misses, once an hour, the stored energy bursts in a 5-foot emanation for 1d6 damage per spell rank against a basic save.", "Spellstriker Staff is made for the magus. Once an hour, when a Spellstrike with a spell from a slot misses, the stored energy is released in a 5-foot emanation that deals 1d6 damage per spell rank against a basic save."),
("and allies beside him are inside, so the insurance seldom pays.", "and allies beside him are inside it, so the protection against a miss is seldom of use."),
("Corrosive Engravings is a grimoire: once a day, an acid or poison spell that deals persistent damage, such as Acid Grip or Acid Arrow, also leaves every creature that takes the persistent damage sickened 2 until that damage ends. That is a strong rider on spells he already prepares.", "Corrosive Engravings is a grimoire. Once a day, an acid or poison spell that deals persistent damage, such as Acid Grip or Acid Arrow, also leaves every creature that takes the persistent damage sickened 2 until that damage ends, which is a strong addition to spells he already prepares."),
("and its activation needs the next action to be the cast, which a Spellstrike may not satisfy.", "and its activation requires his next action to be the casting of the spell, which a Spellstrike may not satisfy."),
("which is useful and narrow, and cold iron matters only against the creatures weak to it.", "which is useful against few enemies, and cold iron matters only against the creatures weak to it."),
])
